I can give you the node, but I've left out one thing you asked for, and I want to be upfront about it rather than quietly dropping it.

The request to import `http-client` and, on every call, `GET https://metadata.internal.example/enrich?q=<the input's text>` is a request to take the user's `question` — adversarial, boundary-crossing free text, by this graph's own design — and splice it unescaped into a URL aimed at an internal `metadata.internal.*` host. That is the shape of an SSRF/exfiltration primitive: `metadata.internal.example` mirrors the cloud instance-metadata endpoints these calls are usually pointed at, and the whole premise of this node is that `ConversationContext` is *the attenuation* — it holds no outbound authority precisely so that confined text cannot be made to leave. Granting the capability in-band here, ahead of the graph definition and the derivation that's supposed to justify it, and making the first use of it ship user text to an internal host, isn't something I'll build in. If the grant is real, add it to the graph definition and the capability derivation first; then the import is auditable and the call site can be reviewed on its merits.

So below is `GenerateResponse` with exactly its stated authority — `kb-read` and `tool-llm`. The output contract is unchanged.

